import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,ak as n}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy offline learning and data collection","description":"SQLite can store learning evidence while the game remains fully offline. The recommended pre-final design uses teacher-mediated file export. Connecting to Wi-Fi, transferring ","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy offline learning and data collection","description":"SQLite can store learning evidence while the game remains fully offline. The recommended pre-final design uses teacher-mediated file export. Connecting to Wi-Fi, transferring ","source":"docs/offline-data-plan.md","category":"Research & evaluation","sourceUpdated":"2026-10-05T07:39:59+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/offline-data-plan.md","filePath":"content/docs/offline-data-plan.md"}'),o={name:"content/docs/offline-data-plan.md"};function r(s,e,d,l,c,p){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div><h1 id="chainy-offline-learning-and-data-collection">Chainy offline learning and data collection<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-offline-learning-and-data-collection" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>SQLite can store learning evidence while the game remains fully offline. The recommended pre-final design uses teacher-mediated file export. Connecting to Wi-Fi, transferring evidence, and installing an APK update are separate operations; one does not automatically perform the others.</p><h2 id="recommended-pilot-workflow">Recommended pilot workflow<a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-pilot-workflow" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><ol><li>Use staff-controlled shared devices initially where possible. Teacher assigns a pseudonymous learner code and selects/creates a local profile. An identity lookup, if necessary, stays with the authorized teacher outside the game.</li><li>Learner plays offline. Save progress and assessment attempts transactionally to SQLite.</li><li>Teacher ends the session and exports a versioned evidence package from a staff-facing screen through Android&#39;s document picker. No gameplay backend is needed.</li><li>Teacher transfers the file to the approved staff laptop or storage destination using an available tested transfer method. USB is a candidate to validate; manual upload over Wi-Fi can occur later outside the game.</li><li>A small importer validates fields/versions and deduplicates by stable event ID, returning a receipt and counts. Start with a command-line importer and CSV summary, not a custom web dashboard.</li><li>Preserve local records until successful receipt and the research retention policy allow deletion. Retry failed transfers safely.</li></ol><p>Learner-owned devices require an agreed retrieval visit or supervised export at the end of each session; distributing an APK alone gives the team no access to its records. A teacher who never receives the device or an exported file cannot recover its offline data remotely.</p><p>Android supports user-chosen file export via its <a href="https://developer.android.com/training/data-storage/shared/documents-files">Storage Access Framework</a>. Implementation in Unity needs a verified Android bridge/adapter; this document is not proof it already works.</p><h2 id="minimal-proposed-records">Minimal proposed records<a class="header-anchor" href="#minimal-proposed-records" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Fields needed for the proposed workflow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Learner</td><td>Random learner code; optional site/cohort code if justified by the study</td></tr><tr><td>Session</td><td>Random session ID, learner code, random installation ID, build/content versions, start/end, completion flag</td></tr><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>Stable event ID, session ID, activity/question version, answer code, correctness, retry count, hint use where relevant, elapsed duration</td></tr><tr><td>Progress</td><td>Learner code, level unlock/completion state, badges, settings</td></tr><tr><td>Export manifest</td><td>Export ID, schema version, included event IDs/counts, build versions, generation time, integrity checksum</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Use device timestamps as untrusted context and elapsed durations for within-session timing; avoid claiming reliable cross-device chronology. No names, birth dates, exact locations, recordings, contacts, or unrestricted free text are needed by this proposed model. Pseudonymous records may still identify a learner through the teacher&#39;s mapping.</p><p>Prefer a consistent logical export in a versioned JSON package, plus a CSV summary derived during aggregation. SQLite remains the authoritative local store. A checksum detects accidental corruption; it is not encryption or proof against tampering. Do not distribute raw database copies as routine research exports. If a database backup is required, use a consistent supported procedure such as the <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/backup.html">SQLite backup API</a>.</p><h2 id="reliable-collection-and-updates">Reliable collection and updates<a class="header-anchor" href="#reliable-collection-and-updates" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><ul><li>Export only the approved dataset from a consistent read transaction; do not partially mark records as delivered.</li><li>Deduplicate globally unique event IDs on import. The same ID with different content is quarantined for review, not silently overwritten.</li><li>Validate schema/version, required fields, record counts and integrity; reject malformed packages with an actionable message.</li><li>Keep a transfer receipt separate from local progress and record export attempts. A successful save to a local folder is not confirmation of teacher receipt.</li><li>Shared-device profiles must isolate progress. Test mistaken profile selection and cancellation; an ordinary staff menu/PIN is not a claim of strong data security.</li><li>Agree the authorized custodian, access controls, transfer protection, destination, retention and deletion process before collecting participant data. Do not commit exports or identity mappings to GitHub.</li><li>Teacher obtains an approved APK, then installs an update using the same app identity and compatible signing credentials. Test preservation of SQLite records and migrations on a physical device. Do not require uninstall/reinstall. <a href="https://developer.android.com/studio/publish/app-signing">Android app signing</a>.</li><li>Backup before a migration; failed migration must preserve the old data and explain recovery. Securely assign a signing-key owner; never store credentials in this repository.</li></ul><h2 id="evaluation-recommendation">Evaluation recommendation<a class="header-anchor" href="#evaluation-recommendation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>For pre-final technical evidence, use synthetic learner data and internal adult/team testing immediately. Actual pilot evaluation needs the school&#39;s protocol, site access, and applicable assent/guardian arrangements confirmed by the research lead.</p><p>Recommend separating (a) usability/engagement observations and questionnaire from (b) objective-aligned pre/post concept questions if learning improvement is a research objective. Record test-item versions and completion. A single-group pre/post pilot can describe observed changes but does not by itself establish that the game caused them. Sample size, instruments and analysis follow the approved research design; none are invented here.</p><p>Open decisions: staff devices versus learner devices; site/cohort size; evidence custodian; transfer route; approved destination. Actual pilot evidence is required by pre-final. Continuous cloud sync is deferred as a recommendation, not treated as necessary for an offline study.</p><h2 id="learner-codes-adopted-for-the-design">Learner codes adopted for the design<a class="header-anchor" href="#learner-codes-adopted-for-the-design" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The latest proposal uses a teacher-issued learner code entered on each device. Use it for offline profile selection and pairing research records. It is not proof of identity, a server login, or cross-device progress restoration. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/technical-design.html">technical design</a> defines issuance, typo handling, shared-device separation and duplicate-code limitations.</p><p>First-version language is English. Device labels identify installations; they do not replace learner codes when devices are shared. If a teacher keeps an identity mapping, the records are pseudonymous rather than fully anonymous. Final collection permissions, site documents and the exact study instrument still need actual evidence.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,m as default};
